Normalization Checklist
 1NF: All columns contain atomic values (no repeating groups)
 2NF: No partial dependencies (all non-key attributes depend on entire primary key)
 3NF: No transitive dependencies (non-key attributes don't depend on other non-key attributes)
 Referential Integrity: Foreign keys properly reference primary keys
 Data Redundancy: Minimal data duplication
Troubleshooting
Error: relation already exists

-- Drop tables in reverse order (due to foreign keys)
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS enrollments;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS courses;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS instructors;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS students;
Error: foreign key constraint violation

Ensure referenced records exist before inserting dependent records
Insert data in order: instructors → courses → students → enrollments
Error: syntax error near...

Check for missing commas between column definitions
Ensure all statements end with semicolons
Verify data type syntax
Conclusion
In this lab, you practiced essential database design skills:

Entity Identification: Extracted entities and attributes from business requirements
Relationship Modeling: Defined cardinality and relationships between entities
Normalization: Applied 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF to eliminate redundancy and anomalies
Schema Implementation: Created normalized tables with proper constraints
Key Takeaways:

Normalization reduces data redundancy and prevents update anomalies
Foreign keys enforce referential integrity between related tables
Junction tables resolve many-to-many relationships
Proper schema design improves data consistency and query performance
